om/en/places/pun-thao/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/pun-thao/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="ตะกั่วป่า 101 หน้าแรก"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/pun-thao/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="เปิดเมนู"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="ค้นหาในเว็บไซต์ (กด Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="ทริปของคุณ"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</h1><p class="english">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">5 verified items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="pun-thao">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/pun-thao.webp" alt="ศาลเจ้าปุนเถ่ากงอ๊า" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8313123, 98.3684438 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8313123,98.3684438" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://goo.gl/maps/JREsvAnLQ2vycGG78" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A shrine from the Chinese community in a mining port</h3><p class="era-years">The construction year is unknown; it was probably built during the formation of Takua Pa's old-town community, but the year cannot yet be specified</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Official evidence from Takua Pa Municipality identifies Bun Thao Kong A Shrine as the town's first shrine and places it in the old port district known as Chap Se or Chap Sao. This locates the shrine within Takua Pa's history of waterborne trade and old-market activity, but the documents found give neither a construction start year nor the name of an individual founder</p><p>The Talat Yai Community database identifies the builders as Chinese immigrants who came to Takua Pa to mine, mentioning groups such as Hokkien, Hainanese, and Cantonese. This presents the shrine as both a ritual site and an anchor for the workers and merchants of a mining town. However, there is not enough direct evidence to determine which group founded it or what specific role the shrine played in the port system</p><p>The municipality also records an incense burner “presented by King Rama VI”. This is evidence of a past relationship between the local shrine and the Siamese state, but the year, occasion, and provenance of the object are not specified</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan Fiscal Year 2020 (2563 BE)</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>An old shrine with gaps in its transition records</h3><p class="era-years">The year of restoration or any ownership change is unknown; activity records exist from at least 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Among the verifiable public sources, no record has been found that the shrine was abandoned, changed hands, or restored in any particular year, and no ancient-monument registration announcement has been found for this shrine. Such details should therefore not be inferred from the context of the old town or other shrines</p><p>What can be verified is that in 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564), a video recorded the Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin or Pae Kong at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine in Chap Se, while another video mentions the oil-bathing ceremony, Phra La ceremony, and bringing in the sacred fire. This shows that the shrine was still a ritual space for Takua Pa descendants at that time</p><p>As for whether the 2004 tsunami (พ.ศ. 2547) affected the building, and the effects of tin-mine closures and economic change in the town, no source has been found that addresses this shrine directly. This is recorded as a research gap, without concluding whether damage occurred</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai: Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin, Pae Kong, at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai: Oil-bathing and Phra La ceremonies at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine</a> · <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fine Arts Department, Ancient Monuments Database</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Still alive as a community shrine, but 2026 status data is incomplete</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>The latest evidence found does not verify opening hours, an official custodian, an admission fee, or the building's condition in 2026 (พ.ศ. 2569). It should therefore not be written that the shrine is open to tourists every day. What can be verified is that it is on Udom Thara Road in Talat Yai Community and has a history connected to the old port district of Chap Se</p><p>In terms of community use, a 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564) video shows Chinese ceremonies taking place and Takua Pa descendants returning to join the activities. The shrine therefore still appears to be a place of faith and shared community memory, but there is not enough public evidence to identify an annual event calendar or a management committee</p><p>No news from the past five years has been found confirming a restoration project, award, or development project specifically for this shrine, and no current image from an official agency with verifiable usage rights is available</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PaiPaPai (2021 activity video)</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">21 September 2021 (พ.ศ. 2564)</p><h3>Birthday ceremony and faith activities at the shrine</h3><p>There is a video recording the birthday ceremony of Hok Tek Chia Sin / Pae Kong at Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine, Chap Se.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">2021 (2564 BE)</p><h3>Oil bathing ceremony, Phra La, and inviting the sacred fire</h3><p>Another video mentions the shrine's rituals and the return of Takua Pa descendants to join the event.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Start of shrine construction</h3><p>The construction year and founder's name are not yet found; official sources identify it as the first shrine in Takua Pa town.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Incense burner bestowed by King Rama VI</h3><p>The municipality states there is an incense burner bestowed by King Rama VI, but does not specify the year or occasion.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Incense burner linked to King Rama VI</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Municipal documents state that there is a royally bestowed incense burner inside the shrine. There is no verified information on its display location or public viewing availability today.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Principal deity Pun Thao Kong</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A travel article states that the deity Pun Thao Kong is the principal deity inside. This information is not verified by the shrine or official agencies.</p><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Chap Se shophouses on Udom Thara Road</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A viewing spot in the area around the shrine, not inside it: The community database mentions two-story old buildings on Udom Thara Road in the Chap Se district.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2021-10-28 · PaiPaPai</p><h3>Birthday of Hok Tek Chia Sin (Pae Kong), Pun Thao Kong Am Shrine, Chap Se, Takua Pa town</h3><p>A news video/recording of the shrine's birthday ceremony activities.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Takua Pa Town Municipality states that this shrine is the first shrine of Takua Pa town.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Inside the shrine is an incense burner that municipal documents state was bestowed by King Rama VI.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Chap Se area shophouses, Udom Thara Road <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Old town wall camp, Udom Thara Road <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/guan-yu/">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/rong-jae/">The Shrine in the Garden</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Municipal documents state that Bun Thao Kong A Shrine was the first shrine in Takuapa and is located in the old port district known as Chap Se or Chap Sao. The municipality records that the shrine contains an incense burner presented by King Rama VI, but does not specify the year or occasion of the presentation.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>A travel article states that the shrine's principal deity is Pun Thao Kong. This has not been verified by the shrine or an official agency. A sight in the area around the shrine, not inside it: the community database mentions a two-storey old building on Udom Thara Road in Chap Se</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>Documents mention the shrine in Takua Pa's old port district</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Municipality, Environmental Action Plan for fiscal year 2020 (พ.ศ. 2563), document pages 11 and 13</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre, Talat Yai Community Database</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dash MV, Phang Nga and Takua Pa destinations, cultural attractions</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Google Maps, the shrine pin linked from the Dash MV article</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://goo.gl/maps/JREsvAnLQ2vycGG78" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan Fiscal Year 2020 (2563 BE)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) Talat Yai community database</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dash MV, cultural tourism article</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://dashmv.com/blog-posts/cultural-tourism" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">PaiPaPai: Sae Yit ceremony for Phra Hok Tek Jiah Sin, Pae Kong</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkMXN_xPdLI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">PaiPaPai: Oil-bathing and Phra La ceremonies</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8El59smf1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The main Thai and English names are according to the brief. No readable sources have been found using the exact English name; alternative names cite municipal documents and a travel article, while SEO keywords are suggestions for searching, not evidence of official names.</li><li>Coordinates were read from a Google Maps redirect link attached to an article. The destination uses the name Bun Tao Kong Shrine. The destination can be opened, but there is no independent second map or confirming sign photo, so confidence is medium.</li><li>The road and community are cited from the religious sites list in municipal documents, while the subdistrict is cited from the Talat Yai page of the Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre. No specific building number or postal code was found.</li><li>No opening hours and admission fees specific to this shrine were found, so it is not concluded that it is open every day or free to enter.</li><li>No phone number, Facebook page, website, or LINE verified as a direct channel for this shrine was found.</li><li>No directions to the shrine from Takua Pa town center, Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport with verifiable distances were found.</li><li>No construction year, individual founder name, restoration year, or evidence of specific roles in the tin industry outside of being founded by Chinese miners was found.</li><li>No evidence directly classifying the architectural style of the shrine building was found, so the Sino-Portuguese description of buildings in the community is not used for the shrine.</li><li>No ancient monument registration announcement specifying this shrine directly was found.</li><li>No suitable visiting times, duration, dress codes, parking, restrooms, ramps, or accessibility information for the elderly and wheelchairs were found.</li><li>There is not yet enough information on things to see inside the shrine to complete 3-6 items whose current condition can be verified, so two items are given based on source descriptions and one item in the area around the shrine.</li><li>Search results found a video of the Sae Yid ceremony for Hok Tek Chia Sin dated September 21, 2021, but the page could not be opened, so it is not used to confirm the festival, month, or lunar calendar.</li><li>Many search results for the Vegetarian Festival relating to the Bun Tao Kong Shrine in Khuekkhak Subdistrict or the Guan Yu Shrine cannot be used as a schedule for the Chap Se district shrine.</li><li>The nearby location has evidence that it is on Udom Thara Road in the same district, but actual walking routes and times have not been verified.</li><li>No official image link clearly identifying this shrine with usage rights was found.</li><li>The municipal plan cited is the Environmental Operations Plan for Fiscal Year 2020, not the original brochure. Historical text in the community database is similar to the municipal document, so they are not considered two independent sources.</li><li>The exact construction year, individual founder names, and specific reasons for construction have not found evidence.</li><li>No restoration year, change of hands, abandonment, or ancient monument registration has been found yet.</li><li>No evidence has been found yet on how the shrine was affected by the 2004 tsunami or the closure of the tin mines.</li><li>Caretakers, opening hours, admission fees, building conditions, and annual activities in 2026 are not verified.</li><li>There are no verifiable old photos from archives or local historical sources published online.</li><li>Current photos found are sample images from video covers, not image files from official agencies, and require permission before use.</li><li>No news from the past five years regarding specific restoration projects, awards, or development projects for the shrine has been found beyond the 2021 activity videos.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="กลับขึ้นด้านบน">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="เมนูด่วน"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
